# manual-checks.allowlist -- check scripts that legitimately CANNOT run in
# check-contracts.sh, each with the reason.
#
# UNWIRED-CHECK-CLOSE-1 (2026-07-30). Enforced by check-contract-coverage.py:
# every scripts/check-*.{py,sh} must be either wired into check-contracts.sh
# or listed here WITH A REASON. A row with no reason fails the coverage check,
# because an unexplained exemption is indistinguishable from an oversight --
# and an oversight here is exactly how 21 checks went dark and 6 of them sat
# red for weeks with nobody watching.
#
# Format:  <script-name>: <reason it cannot run in the seconds-scale aggregator>
#
# Being on this list is NOT permission to rot. Each row states what would have
# to change for the script to become wirable, so the exemption is falsifiable.

# --- needs a compiler / build ---------------------------------------------
check-editor-build.sh: Compiles and links the EditRel + mc2_asset_viewer targets; minutes, not seconds. Wirable only behind a separate heavy-gate runner.
check-frame-graph.py: Drives a standalone cmake configure+build of tests/unit (VS2022). Its invocation was broken (exit 127 on every run) until UNWIRED-CHECK-CLOSE-1 fixed the WSL-bash/backslash defect; it is now genuinely runnable, but still a build, so it belongs to the unit-test lane, not the static aggregator. DARK-TESTS-RUN-1 (2026-07-31) was that lane and took the handoff: it removed the `--ts=FrameGraph,ViewCurrency` filter that made this the only doctest driver in the repo while STILL excluding TEST_SUITE("RenderCore") -- the suite whose count lock had drifted 46-vs-62 through fifteen appends. It now runs the full doctest tier via --doctest-only. It stays exempt because it is still a build, and only a build. MEASURED, so the exemption is falsifiable rather than assumed: configure 1.7s + warm incremental build 0.69s + full 631-case run 0.22s = ~2.6s warm, but ~2-3 MINUTES cold, because build64-tests/ exists in none of the 52 worktrees. The warm number is inside this aggregator's ~5s promise and the cold number is not, so wiring it would make the suite's first run in each worktree pay minutes -- and it needs a compiler, which no other wired row does. TRIGGER for wiring: a heavy-gate runner that owns a warm build64-tests, or a decision that a compiler is an acceptable dependency for the static board.

# --- needs a cooked asset tree --------------------------------------------
check-texture-resolution.py: Validates a COOKED texture inventory and emits cook.json provenance; requires a deployed cook tree (default A:/Games/mc2-opengl/mc2-win64-v0.4) that does not exist in a source checkout. Its 100 FAILs were NOT asset defects -- every one was cook SCRATCH under _aoktxtmp/ (the transcoder temp dir made by tools/bt2018_import/cook_ao_from_package.py), which the walk now skips; it reports FAIL=0 over 8053 real cooked textures. TRIGGER for wiring: a runner that owns a deployed tree, e.g. the deploy/cook lane.
check-ktx2-probe.py: Bakes and inspects a KTX2 probe; writes artifacts under out/asset-pipeline-probe. Side-effecting, so it must not run inside a read-only aggregator.

# --- needs a deployed release tree ----------------------------------------
check-icon-atlases.py: Scans deployed release dirs under A:/Games/mc2-opengl/releases/ for icon-atlas size agreement. No deploy tree in a source checkout. NOTE: currently prints "!! FLAG: size disagreement" yet exits 0 -- report-only. Wiring it requires first deciding whether that flag is a failure.
check-ab-kit.py: Grades the A/B batch kit inside a DEPLOYED INSTALL, supplied as its one positional argument (DST = sys.argv[1]). Nothing it reads is in the repo -- no AB-*.bat is tracked on any branch, so a clone has nothing for it to parse and BOTH entry conditions raise: IndexError on sys.argv[1] with no argument, and IndexError on the AB-0 baseline lookup against a directory with no kit. Wiring it means hard-coding an install name that encodes the day it was cut (A:/Games/MC2-OpenGL-FINAL-2026-07-31 today), and that row is red on every clone forever and red on this disk the moment the directory is renamed or superseded -- the permanently-red wallpaper this file already refuses twice (check-flag-registry-drift.sh, check-env-allowlist.sh). The obvious escape, skip-when-absent, is worse: it is structurally GREEN in every clone, i.e. a report wearing a gate's colour, which is exactly what DELIVERY-CLOSE-1 refused for check-delivery.py's install half. MEASURED 2026-07-31 so the exemption is falsifiable rather than assumed -- `py -3 scripts/check-ab-kit.py "A:/Games/MC2-OpenGL-FINAL-2026-07-31"` reports `9 arms checked, 0 failing`, exit 0; injection-proven to exit 1 in RECORD-TRUTH-1 (an arm's set line edited to contradict its own filename). The shipped copy at <install>/verify-ab-kit.py is byte-identical to this one (sha256 27b5d2c191b9e648b147e1ce8cf8a7d0ff63123ac2861902ac027c2af3da7f4f), so grading the install grades this file. TRIGGER for wiring: the kit stops being hand-written into an install and becomes a GENERATED artifact (an emitter under scripts/ or tools/ that deploy_payload.py drives), at which point the input tree is in-repo, clone-independent and wirable against the generator's own output; or a deploy-lane runner that owns exactly one install by contract.

# --- needs a live engine run ----------------------------------------------
check-ambient-guard.py: Reads latest_render_state.json from a live engine run (MC2_DEBUG_STATE_DUMP=1). Same class as the header-documented check-debug-state-json.py exclusion.
check-debug-state-json.py: Requires a live engine JSON snapshot. Already named as an explicit exclusion in the check-contracts.sh header; this row makes that exclusion machine-readable instead of a comment.

# --- blocked on a clean tree ----------------------------------------------
check-flag-registry-drift.sh: STAYS ADVISORY, and the reason is now measured rather than provisional (REGISTRY-PARSER-TRUTH-1, 2026-07-31). This row used to say "TRIGGER: once RendererFeatureRegistry.h and tier1_env_vars.md are committed, regenerate and wire --strict". That was tried, and --strict is the wrong wiring for TWO reasons the old note could not have known. (1) IT CANNOT CATCH THE CLASS OF BUG THAT MATTERED: it regenerates flags.auto.yaml with the same generator and diffs, so while that generator was parsing 248 of the header's 360 rows this check read "up to date" the whole time -- a wrong-but-stable generator is invisible to a self-diff. The 73 false `registered: no` verdicts sat in the tracked file from 2026-07-23 with this script present and silent. (2) IT IS NOT STABLE ON A SHARED WORKTREE: it sweeps the WORKING TREE for getenv() sites and records `file:line`, so any other lane's uncommitted C++ edit that shifts a line number makes it red -- observed going red within minutes of being wired, from unrelated edits in gos_terrain_lod_chunk.cpp and mech3d.cpp. A permanently-red row teaches everyone to skip red. The claim that actually needed guarding is now wired as `flag_registry_truth` (scripts/check-flag-registry-truth.py), which grades HEAD's registry against the committed doc -- both committed artifacts, so it is immune to any lane's in-flight work and goes red only when the doc is genuinely wrong. Keep this one advisory for the broader byte-level drift it does report well.
check-brain-opord.py: Runs a mission headless against a staged mod and asserts runtime BRAIN_* traces. Requires --exe plus a staged carver_probe mod; also blocked outright while game launches are hard-blocked.
check_vk_nointerop_log.py: Grades a Vulkan no-interop smoke LOG supplied as a positional argument. Branch-specific (claude/vk-interop-bridge-1) and log-dependent.

# --- UNWIRED-CHECKS-CLOSE-1 (2026-07-30) ----------------------------------
# The rows above were written while check-contract-coverage.py arm (b) still
# credited a check for being NAMED in a comment. Nine checks were therefore
# never offered to this file at all. Arm (b) is now invocation-shaped; these
# are the ones that genuinely cannot run in a seconds-scale static aggregator.

# --- needs a live engine run (all three launch mc2.exe) -------------------
check-combat-soak.py: Requires --exe and drives a real timed mission to grade combat oracles from the run's JSONL. Same class as check-brain-opord.py. Also blocked outright while game launches are hard-blocked. TRIGGER for wiring: a soak lane that owns a deployed exe.
check-file-resolve-miss.py: Requires --exe; runs a mission with MC2_LOG_FILE_RESOLVE=2 and diffs the MISS trace against scripts/file-resolve-miss-baseline.txt. Designed as a NIGHTLY audit, not a per-commit gate. Its only mention in run_smoke.py is a COMMENT explaining why two env vars sit in the passthrough allowlist -- that comment is what falsely credited it as wired.
check-order-liveness.py: Requires --exe; asserts runtime order-execution traces appear in a live mission. TRIGGER: the same exe-owning lane as check-combat-soak.py.

# --- report-only: wiring either mode would be dishonest -------------------
check-env-allowlist.sh: ADJUDICATION, not deferral. Default mode ALWAYS exits 0 and --strict exits 1 TODAY on 8 real harness misses (MC2_DIAGNOSTIC_TRACE_FILE, MC2_SMOKE_REQUIRE_{DIAG_STATE,FINGERPRINT,FRESH,VISUAL}, MC2_SMOKE_VISUAL_REPORT, MC2_SOAK_AUTOWIN, MC2_VAR). So default-wiring adds a row that can never go red and --strict-wiring adds one that is red on arrival -- a permanently-red gate teaches everyone to ignore the board, which is the same blindness in a different colour. The misses are real and belong to the smoke-harness owner, not to a contracts lane: each is a var run_smoke.py sets on the child directly while the passthrough allowlist does not list it, and deciding whether the fix is "add to allowlist" or "stop setting it directly" needs that lane's intent. TRIGGER: once those 8 are adjudicated, wire as `sh scripts/check-env-allowlist.sh --strict`.
check-worktree-context.sh: Interactive orientation aid for a HUMAN starting work in the wrong worktree -- it prints which worktree/branch you are in and what that implies. It has no invariant to assert and no failure mode; scripts/new_lane.py only ECHOES its signature and prints "Run `sh scripts/check-worktree-context.sh` before editing", which is advice, not a call. Kept because that advice is load-bearing in a 30-worktree repo, but it is a command a person runs, not a contract. It is NOT a candidate for deletion and NOT a candidate for wiring.
